DS MAP: export the full map state and expose command bridges so the companion can replace the
native MapWindow on the app-controlled maximize path, and suppress that window while it does.

SCOPE IS DELIBERATELY NARROW. The DS map replaces the native map ONLY while its overlay is mounted,
which happens only from the companion minimap tap (CMP:openmap). The combined GM_Inventory view
(map alongside Inventory/Spells/Stats) and the pinned HUD map are untouched -- the map is one of
four windows sharing GM_Inventory, so suppressing the MODE would have broken the four-pane layout,
and the companion already has its own minimap for the pinned case.

Four things are load-bearing:

  * THE SUPPRESSION FLAG IS MOUNT-SCOPED, NOT A ONE-SHOT. companionDsMapActive() is
    (element == DS) && (overlay currently mounted); Kotlin sets the mount half when the overlay
    appears and clears it when it goes away, and updateVisible() reads the live value. A one-shot
    armed when CMP:openmap is drained was rejected: that open can fail to happen at all (char-gen
    suppression, a close toggle), and the stale flag would then suppress a LATER map open that came
    from a vanilla path -- the same lifetime problem g_companionPendingMapActive already had to
    solve. Exactly ONE window is gated, mMap, at both of its setVisible sites.

  * THE STATE IS PUSHED WHOLE, ONCE PER MOUNT -- no live deltas. GM_Inventory is a GUI mode, so the
    sim is PAUSED for as long as the DS map is up: the player cannot move, no cell can be explored,
    no fog texel can change, and no marker can appear except through the note commands here (each
    of which re-exports). A full snapshot is therefore not merely simpler, it is complete.
    CONSEQUENTLY NOTHING HOOKS GlobalMap::copyResult. That hook would have been the live overlay
    delta, and it is the one place in this design where a mistake leaks render cameras (copyResult
    controls camera cleanup via its return value and its erase). Not needing it removes that risk
    entirely rather than managing it.

  * NEITHER LARGE PAYLOAD NEEDS A GPU READBACK. Both global map layers are already CPU-side images:
    mBaseTexture carries its osg::Image, and mOverlayImage is a CPU copy the engine maintains
    deliberately (exploreCell passes cpuCopy=true because write() PNG-encodes it into the save). Fog
    is likewise authored on the CPU in LocalMap::updatePlayer and never rendered. So this is all
    memcpy -- unlike the local map export's draw-callback + glReadPixels.

  * FOG IS SENT ALPHA-ONLY. LocalMap::updatePlayer writes `val = alpha << 24` and never touches RGB,
    so three quarters of a 32x32 RGBA segment is provably zero: 1024 bytes per segment instead of
    4096, landing straight in an ALPHA_8 Bitmap.

The World/Local toggle is deliberately NOT bridged. MapWindow::onWorldButtonClicked writes
Settings::map().mGlobal, a persisted device-wide setting -- flipping the DS map's view would have
silently and permanently changed the native map's own view state for a player who prefers it. The
DS map owns its local/global choice entirely in Compose and writes nothing native.

Discovered-location markers are exported ALREADY CLUSTERED, walked from mGlobalMapMarkers (the
result of addVisitedLocation's barycentre aggregation) rather than re-derived on the Kotlin side --
the same reasoning as the Enchanting effect display line.

EditNoteDialog is gated inside its own setVisible(), so every show path is covered rather than just
the call sites -- it is a WindowModal outside any GuiModeState, the same class of problem
Enchanting's four modals had.

Measured on the real load order (Morrowind+Tribunal+Bloodmoon): global map 954x864, base 2.36 MB,
overlay 3.14 MB, ~6.3 MB peak for a single-shot base. Tiling would only be needed past roughly
1400x1400, which takes a worldspace-expanding mod (e.g. Tamriel Rebuilt) to reach.

Kotlin side: LogParser prefixes + three binary JNI deliveries -> MapDsSession in
GameStateRepository -> MapDsTopOverlay (top screen) and MapDsControls (bottom). Added Aug 20 2026.

